The #627 fix is queued with the manager again, on top of current main, but it is unverified: no build runner took my full-gates build within 15 minutes, so there is no verdict yet.

**The conflict:** main changed `cross-test/memberport/testdata/MemberPortStreamExpiryTest.java` in #919 (commit 57292992). It now handles a slow-starting stream by trying again on a token twice as long. My branch handled the same case differently, by opening a stream with no token first to warm the connection. I kept main's approach and dropped that warm-up. Of everything in this change, the warm-up was the part I most suspected of causing the earlier batch builds that hung, so removing it also removes that suspect. What stays on top of main in that file is a single 5 s limit on how long after its token's expiry a stream may take to close (it was 1.5 s in one check and 4.5 s in another).

**The commit, 3a9cd32**, contains the same fix as before:
- **Java stream-expiry test:** each handler gets 15 s after the stream closes to see its call end, instead of a fixed 3 s. A failure says how long it waited. A handler that never ran (the stream was refused when it opened) is reported as that rather than as a late cancellation. A new test checks that a listener attached after the call was already cancelled still runs.
- **Rust stream-expiry test:** the control stream may go 5 s without a message before it counts as stalled, up from 2 s, and the failure says how long it waited.
- **Go:** no change needed, because main's Go test already measures this by elapsed time.

**Still to do in the next run, after the manager's verdict:**
- **If a batch hangs again:** the trace line in the build log names the leg that was still running, and that leg gets fixed.
- **If it merges:**
  - Update backport PRs #636 to #642 to cite main's commit in place of 7cf43dbb/74307086, noting that the Go part and the memberport file differ on main.
  - Comment the outcome on the issue (gate set, batch; no handlers are touched by this change; the trace-check findings belong to #311, which is still open), then close the issue.

There is no v2.6.2 tag from main, because main has taken features since v2.6.1; the patch ships through the release-branch backports.
